Here's my performance analysis for `DataSize.cs` on your **hot path** (thousands of concurrent transfers, real-time formatting):

---

## Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.Replace(` | 7 |
| `.Contains(` without `StringComparison` | 2 (L173) |
| `.Contains(` with `StringComparison` | 2 (L212, L218) ✅ |
| `CompareInfo.IndexOf` (culture-sensitive search) | 1 |
| String interpolation `$"..."` | 2 |
| Unsealed classes | 0 (struct — ✅) |
| `IEquatable<T>` on struct | 1 ✅ |
| LINQ/Regex/async | 0 |

---

## Findings

#### 1. Branched `string.Replace()` chains in `ToString` (7 instances)
**Impact:** Each `Replace` allocates a new string. On a hot path called thousands of times/sec, this creates significant GC pressure. Up to 2 Replace calls per invocation (L179 + one branch).
**Files:** DataSize.cs:L179, L189, L195, L201, L207, L214, L220
**Fix:** Eliminate `Replace` entirely — the unit symbols (`TB`, `GB`, etc.) are already the same as the replacement values. Restructure to select the numeric value and append the symbol separately, avoiding all intermediate string allocations:

```csharp
public string ToString(string? format, IFormatProvider? formatProvider = null)
{
    format ??= "0.##";
    formatProvider ??= CultureInfo.CurrentCulture;

    var (value, symbol) = GetUnitValueAndSymbol(format, formatProvider);
    return $"{value.ToString(format, formatProvider)} {symbol}";
}

private (double Value, string Symbol) GetUnitValueAndSymbol(string format, IFormatProvider provider)
{
    var culture = provider as CultureInfo ?? CultureInfo.CurrentCulture;
    bool has(string s) => culture.CompareInfo.IndexOf(format, s, CompareOptions.IgnoreCase) != -1;

    if (has(TerabyteSymbol)) return (Terabytes, TerabyteSymbol);
    if (has(GigabyteSymbol)) return (Gigabytes, GigabyteSymbol);
    if (has(MegabyteSymbol)) return (Megabytes, MegabyteSymbol);
    if (has(KilobyteSymbol)) return (Kilobytes, KilobyteSymbol);
    if (format.Contains(ByteSymbol, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return (Bytes, ByteSymbol);
    if (format.Contains(BitSymbol, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return (Bits, BitSymbol);

    return (LargestWholeNumberValue, LargestWholeNumberSymbol);
}
```

#### 2. Culture-sensitive `CompareInfo.IndexOf` on hot path (1 instance)
**Impact:** Culture-aware comparison is ~5-10x slower than ordinal. For fixed ASCII unit symbols like "KB", "MB", cultural comparison is unnecessary overhead per call.
**Files:** DataSize.cs:L183
**Fix:** Use `StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase` via `format.Contains(s, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)` instead of `CompareInfo.IndexOf`.

#### 3. Redundant precomputed properties stored per instance (5 fields)
**Impact:** Every `DataSize` instance stores `Bytes`, `Kilobytes`, `Megabytes`, `Gigabytes`, `Terabytes` — all derivable from `Bits`. Each arithmetic operation (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`) creates a new instance recomputing all 5 divisions. With thousands of transfers doing rate calculations, this is wasted work and bloats struct size (48 bytes → 8 bytes).
**Files:** DataSize.cs:L42-49, L124-127
**Fix:** Store only `Bits` (or `Bytes`). Compute derived units as read-only properties on demand:

```csharp
public double Bytes => (double)Bits / BitsInByte;
public double Kilobytes => Bytes / BytesInKilobyte;
